WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.084
Zero out of four.

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:09.659
That is Canada's record on international climate targets: Kyoto, Copenhagen, Paris, Net Zero — every single one missed.

00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:14.272
Over one hundred billion dollars in climate spending since 2015.

00:00:14.272 --> 00:00:24.090
Emissions in 2022: approximately six hundred and seventy megatonnes of CO2 equivalent, down from a 2005 baseline of seven hundred and thirty.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:35.920
The Paris target requires reaching four hundred and one to four hundred and thirty-eight megatonnes by 2030 — a reduction of two hundred and thirty-two to two hundred and sixty-nine megatonnes in approximately six years.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.920
The Trans Mountain pipeline: original estimate seven point four billion dollars.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.068
Purchase price in 2018: four point five billion.

00:00:45.068 --> 00:00:50.806
Final cost: thirty-four billion dollars — a three hundred and sixty percent cost overrun.

00:00:50.806 --> 00:00:55.159
The carbon tax generates eight to ten billion dollars per year in revenue.

00:00:55.159 --> 00:01:00.818
The government simultaneously subsidizes fossil fuel infrastructure and taxes its consumption.

00:01:00.818 --> 00:01:04.443
The policy is not contradictory — it is extractive.

00:01:04.443 --> 00:01:09.636
Both sides of the equation generate revenue for the state while emissions continue to rise.
